<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Murad III</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1546">1546</a>–<a href="/years/1595">1595</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-dynasty">Ottoman dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Murad III was sultan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> from 1574 until his death in 1595. Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manisa">Manisa</a> in 1546 to the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selim-ii">Selim II</a> and Nurbanu <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a>, he had his five younger brothers strangled on his accession, and his reign saw the growing influence of the imperial harem, above all his mother and his favourite consort Safiye Sultan. He fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavids</a>, a war of 1578–1590 that brought temporary gains, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> was for a time a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> until 1582. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">Silver</a> from the New World brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inflation">inflation</a> and unrest among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janissaries">Janissaries</a>, while relations with Elizabethan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> were cemented and he commissioned illustrated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> such as the Siyer-i-Nebi.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/murad-iii.jpg" alt="Murad III" width="330" height="586" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 16th century artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Murad_III._Semailname_(Millet_Library,_Ali_Emîrî_Collection,_History,_MS_no._1216).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Murad was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manisa">Manisa</a> on 4 July 1546 to Şehzade Selim and his powerful consort Nurbanu Sultan, and was a grandson of Suleiman I and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hurrem-sultan">Hürrem Sultan</a>. He learned Arabic and Persian, and after his ceremonial circumcision in 1557 Suleiman appointed him governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aksehir">Akşehir</a> in 1558. In 1564 he became governor of Saruhan with Manisa as his seat, and he remained there during his father's reign, continuing his education under the historian Hoca Sadeddin Efendi. Contemporary accounts describe him there as highly educated, talented and deeply religious.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Murad succeeded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selim-ii">Selim II</a> in December 1574. His authority was increasingly influenced by the imperial harem, above all by his mother and later by his favourite consort Safiye Sultan, often at the expense of the Grand Vizier Sokollu Mehmed Pasha, who served until his assassination in October 1579. In 1576 Abd al-Malik and Ramazan Pasha set out from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a> with an army of about 10,000, mostly Ottoman Turks, and restored Abd al-Malik to the Moroccan throne as an Ottoman client. His brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-al-mansur">Ahmad al-Mansur</a> at first recognised Ottoman suzerainty but declared full independence in 1582.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> had been at peace with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavids</a> since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-amasya">Treaty of Amasya</a> of 1555. In 1577 Murad declared war to take advantage of the disorder at the Safavid court after the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tahmasp-i">Shah Tahmasp I</a>, influenced by the viziers Lala Kara Mustafa Pasha and Sinan Pasha and against the counsel of Sokollu. The Ottoman–Safavid War of 1578 to 1590 ended with the Treaty of Constantinople, which brought temporary significant gains. Admiral Mir Ali Beg established Ottoman supremacy in cities on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> coast between Mogadishu and Kilwa, and Ottoman suzerainty was recognised in Mogadishu in 1585.</p><p>By 1580 an influx of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> from the New World had caused high <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inflation">inflation</a> and unrest, especially among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janissaries">Janissaries</a> and officials paid in debased currency. Ottoman and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> sources accuse Murad himself of taking large bribes. He exchanged letters and envoys with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-i">Elizabeth I</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> exported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a>, lead and ammunition to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>. Like his father, Murad never went on campaign; in his last years he did not leave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/topkapi-palace">Topkapı Palace</a>, and the historians Mustafa Âlî and Mustafa Selaniki disapproved of his sedentary life. He commissioned the Siyer-i-Nebi and other illustrated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Murad III” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Michael the Brave, from 1593, attacked <b>Murad III</b>'s troops north and south of the Danube with Sigismund Báthory of Transylvania and Aron Vodă of Moldavia, the campaign of the Battle of Călugăreni.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-wallachia">Principality of Wallachia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…limited production and helped members in hard times, and through which the state watched over the bazaars and the townspeople; by the 1580s the guilds were a fixture of town life, as the account of the great procession of 1582 for the circumcision of <b>Murad III</b>’s son shows.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ottoman-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Ottoman Empire</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manisa">Manisa</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=38.61,27.43&amp;year=1577" aria-label="Manisa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5231 778 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Murad III"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5681 1048h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5681 1048h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=38.61,27.43&amp;year=1577">What was here in 1577? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
